I rebased on 8.2 and pushed two fixes.

**The rename dropped the assertions guarding the client secret on the refresh path.** `OidcConfidentialClientTest::testRefreshToken` asserted `$body['client_secret']` and `testRefreshTokenWithClientSecretBasic` asserted the `Authorization` header plus the absence of the secret from the body. The new `testRefreshToken` keeps `grant_type`, `refresh_token` and `client_id` but not the secret, and the Basic test has no successor; `testTheTokenRequestIsMadeWithTheOptionsTheClientAuthenticationReturns` covers `exchangeCode()` only. So the refresh path had no coverage of the client authentication at all. Measured, on the submitted head, with `refreshToken()` made to skip it:

```
$options = ['body' => $body];   // instead of ->authenticate(...)
OK (1105 tests, 2512 assertions)
```

A refactoring that silently stops sending the client secret on every token refresh would have shipped green. The same mutation fails twice on the base. I restored the assertion and added `testTheRefreshRequestIsMadeWithTheOptionsTheClientAuthenticationReturns`; the mutation now fails both.

**`authenticate()` lost its fourth parameter.** `$options` was dead at both call sites, `OidcClient` passing `[]` each time, so an implementation could only ever receive an empty array while the docblock described "the HttpClient options being built". Worse, the body only reached the request through the returned `$options['body']`, so an implementation that forgot the copy sent a POST with no body, no `Content-Type` and no `Content-Length`, and the author saw `HTTP 400 returned for "https://provider.example.com/token"`, a diagnostic pointing at the provider. That is exactly the mistake the `PrivateKeyJwtAuthentication` example in the description invites.

The signature is now `authenticate(string $clientId, string $tokenEndpoint, array $options): array`, with `OidcClient` passing `['body' => $body]`. The body is then already in the array the implementation receives, so a forgetful implementation returning `$options` untouched still sends it. Nothing outside the three shipped classes implements or calls the interface, and both suites are green. I updated the example in the description to match.

## On the point you flagged for review

The runtime check is fail-closed, which is the part that matters, and I verified it specifically: with a public client and `id_token_signature.required: false`, the request throws before any HTTP call and the security token is untouched, on the eager and the lazy firewall alike, and through a decorated client-authentication service.

But the reason given for not doing better does not survive testing. Of the three cases named, only the decorator genuinely hides the class. `findDefinition()` resolves alias chains at every compiler stage, and a factory cannot defeat it because a factory-built definition without a class does not compile at all: `Please add the class to service "..." even if it is constructed by a factory since we might need to add method calls based on compile-time checks.` A pass at `TYPE_BEFORE_REMOVING` firing only on an exact `NoClientAuthentication` match, falling through to the constructor otherwise, catches every case except the decorator and can only add errors. `SortFirewallListenersPass` sits at that same stage for the same reason. I did not push it, since fail-closed is preserved either way, but it is 53 lines if you want it.

What I would fix regardless is the message. Before, a faulty configuration failed at boot with `Invalid configuration for path "security.firewalls.oidc.oidc_login": The OIDC "pkce.enabled" option cannot be false ...`. Now it fails on the first request with `PKCE cannot be disabled for a public OIDC client`, which names neither the firewall nor a key anyone can grep for in `security.yaml`.

Worth knowing which of the two rules carries the weight: the PKCE one has a backstop, since `NoClientAuthentication` throws anyway when no verifier reaches it. The signature rule has none, so a `none` client with the check off would complete a login on an unverified ID token. That is the one whose coverage matters.

## On the configuration cost

The description presents the choice as service id versus a node growing a branch per profile with keys, algorithms and rotation options frozen at the release. There is a middle ground it does not consider: keep `client_secret` and `token_endpoint_auth_method` as sugar that registers a `ClientSecretBasic` or `ClientSecretPost` service per firewall, with `client_authentication` as the escape hatch, the two mutually exclusive and one required. That is two scalar keys for the two schemes you already ship, not a branch per profile, and it restores compile-time knowledge of the method on the common path for free.

I built it and ran it through the functional app: both sugar shapes boot and reach `/oidc/start`, the conflict and the missing-both cases fail at boot with clear messages, and the public-client shape is untouched. The only thing it breaks is this PR's own `testRejectsAMissingClientAuthentication`. Your call, but it is worth answering rather than leaving, because "the shortest OIDC configuration now declares one service" is what every application will feel.

## The rest

`ClientSecretBasic` is the first conforming `client_secret_basic` in Symfony, and that is worth saying out loud. `auth_basic` base64-encodes its argument verbatim, so a scoped client carrying it misauthenticates any client id containing a colon; I documented that on #65865 and concluded the right home was an OAuth2-aware layer. This is that layer. Verified on the wire: `cli:ent`/`secret` becomes `cli%3Aent:secret`, `clïent`/`sécrét` becomes `cl%C3%AFent:s%C3%A9cr%C3%A9t`. `ClientSecretPost` correctly does not double-encode, since the form encoder encodes once; all seven secret shapes round-trip.

Continuity with #65875 holds in both directions under mutation: the refresh grant still skips the verifier requirement and an authorization-code exchange without one is still refused. The OIDC Core §12.2 `sub` check is intact and still protected. `OidcClient` becoming `final` is safe, nothing extends it anywhere. Both CHANGELOG entries were amended in place rather than appended, which is right for unreleased API, and no UPGRADE entry is owed. `security.oauth2.client_authentication.none` works end to end: a firewall declaring nothing but `provider_uri`, `client_id` and that service id refreshes a token with no `client_secret` in the body and no `Authorization` header.

Two smaller things. `getMethod()` is compared exactly, so `'None'`, `' none'` or any typo silently opts a custom implementation out of both security rules; worth a sentence in the interface docblock saying a security decision reads that value. And the description says the new namespace sits next to `AccessToken\OAuth2\`, where it is actually a new top-level `Security\Http\OAuth2\`, which is arguably the better split but not what the text says.

Documentation the merge owes: the `client_authentication` option and the ready-made service id, the three implementations and when to pick each, and the two rules a public client cannot bend.

